"The ship and the target are {#} t each, far too light to pull on anything, so each moves on its own orbit around the star. A burn changes the ship's velocity at once by Δv (a real burn of minutes is instant on these time scales): forward along its motion around the star, backward against it, outward away from the star or inward toward it. Speeding up lifts the far side of the orbit, and a bigger orbit takes longer (P² = a³/M), so a ship that speeds up to chase a target ahead falls behind it. To catch up, burn backward: the lower orbit is quicker. The dashed line is the ship's path over the next two years (or the next orbit of the target, if longer), worked out with the same n body steps; the crosses mark where the ship and the target are at the closest approach. The ship counts as alongside within {#} AU ({#} {#} km) at under {#} km/s; closing the last stretch takes the same kind of burns, smaller, with the view following the ship. The Hohmann transfer is the half ellipse touching both circles (Hohmann {#}; OpenStax University Physics Vol {#} §{#}).",
